068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1045 "Estimating Derivatives Using a Backward Difference Quotient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186092467 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "21c10e32-8c2e-5d6b-a5cb-19f07aa83fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/40faa5a8b9bc08fcef23477a938c24d9aa6ebbc987b8eaeb3175cdec47bc00b3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58be26c8-7063-5aa8-be3d-09b3c442a08f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092715 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093089 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093089 #edb/ref 1061 22597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093089 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "332d8f20-0643-51b2-8b43-875b7569adc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093089 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093090 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093090 #edb/ref 1061 22598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093090 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0358e8c8-7e96-5bb6-9fc7-eef0c1c3cde8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093090 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093335 #edb/ref 1061 686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5a2b7913-8af1-56e3-ae9c-6dea1ca15630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093336 #edb/ref 1061 687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1579afd-f8b8-522d-815c-72dc7b18e4b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093337 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093337 #edb/ref 1061 691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84a50349-fb71-5a17-b1e7-276d47f34414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093338 #edb/ref 1061 694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093338 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a635f38c-a7e9-57d1-aeb5-e9fe6f8751e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093338 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093444 #edb/ref 1064 "To approximate the derivative of a function, we can use the finite difference formula\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of the step size $h,$ with $h>0.$\n\nThis expression is known as the **backward difference approximation** because if $h>0$ then $x-h$ is a step behind $x$ (i.e., to the left). \n\n![](images/dd/ddc03ba86c8c343d5733b51444cec157182894b6d0c4fccb501b1dc3bca98f57.png)\n\nAs usual, the smaller the step size $h$ is, the better the approximation to $f'(x)$ we obtain." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093444 #edb/ref 1065 368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093444 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6aff8fa6-f787-5984-944a-5648207e6830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093444 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093445 #edb/ref 1064 "Where does the formula for the backward difference come from? Remember that from the definition of derivative, we have the forward difference formula\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of  $h.$ \n\nNow, replacing $h$ with $-h$ in the formula above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x-h)-f(x)}{-h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe step of replacing $h$ with $-h$ is equivalent to stepping a distance of $h$ to the left rather than to the right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093445 #edb/ref 1065 371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093445 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1cc2d207-71d4-53a4-8c67-5af0c5f4d173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093445 #edb/ref 1067 "Derivation of the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315853 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{4}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315853 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315853 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8443d9dc-35f0-5287-a16f-99b3e3adb0ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315943 #edb/ref 1080 "200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315943 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315943 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "942b21b2-98f9-5164-9176-34228edf9f15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316966 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316966 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316966 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316966 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316966 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3a0ff7a-ff90-5202-ab60-d69932d64cb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317125 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317125 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317125 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317125 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4fb7757-9b7b-5def-a523-240ba832d6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1071 "A distance function $s(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.06$ | $0.08$ | $0.10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0.06$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{s}^{′}(0.06)≈$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c44d4d0b-eb44-5f39-a106-5b66e352fb91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317437 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=0.06.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.02.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.06) & ≈\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.06-0.02)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.04)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.02} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fd917228308b839be77110b07e32fa0154350c290920628400bd1f20a34160b5.png)\n\nA factory tracks the number of defective items remaining after a recall. The number of defective items after $x$ days, in hundreds, is given by the function $N(x).$ The graph of $N(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=15$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=10$ and $x=15.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is\n{{field-1}} hundred items per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e6d430f-227b-5241-8f12-1f8ddddf88bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317446 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{N(x)-N(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=15.$  Applying the above formula with $x=15$ and $h=5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{N(15)-N(15-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(15)-N(10)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{72-76}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is $-\\frac{4}{5}$ hundred items per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327747 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327747 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327747 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ca436a4-c1c3-5d20-829e-ecb4501a591b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327993 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327